---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { publicCatalog } from '../lib/catalog.js';
import { url } from '../lib/site.js';

const catalog = publicCatalog();
const tx = catalog.taxonomy;
const natures = tx.natures.map((n) => ({
  ...n,
  count: catalog.paradoxes.filter((p) => p.nature === n.id).length,
}));
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What this site is, who it is for, and what counts as a paradox here."
>
  <article class="ab prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      A collection of the ideas that refuse to sit comfortably in the human head —
      written so that a curious ten-year-old and a working researcher can read
      the same page and both get something out of it.
    </p>

    <h2>One page, many depths</h2>
    <p>
      Every entry is layered. It opens with a story you need no background for,
      then explains what is actually going on in plain language, then goes into
      history, competing interpretations, the technical treatment, and finally
      the original sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      You stop wherever you like. The promise is that everything above where you
      stopped was <em>true</em> — nothing is simplified into something
      misleading. Where a claim needs a caveat to be accurate, the caveat is
      there, in ordinary words.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-counts">What counts as a paradox here</h2>
    <p>
      The word gets used for almost anything surprising, which quietly destroys a
      set of real distinctions. A theorem proving that no voting system can work
      is not the same kind of object as a sentence that contradicts itself, and
      neither is the same as a probability answer that happens to feel wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every entry declares its <a href="/taxonomy/#natures">nature</a>, and
      the site says plainly when something popularly called a paradox is not one:
    </p>

    <dl class="abnat">
      {
        natures.map((n) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {n.count > 0 ? <a href={url.nature(n.id)}>{n.label}</a> : n.label}
              <em>{n.count}</em>
            </dt>
            <dd>{n.explanation}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2>Sources are not decoration</h2>
    <p>
      Every published entry cites its sources, and wherever the original
      formulation is identifiable, the primary source is cited directly.
      References are structured records — author, year, publisher, DOI — shared
      across entries, so the same book cited from six pages is one record that
      can be corrected once. The whole <a href="/sources/">bibliography</a> is
      browsable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where scholarship is uncertain, the text says so. A number of famous origin
      stories in this field are traditions rather than documented history, and
      the entries mark which is which.
    </p>

    <h2>No AI in the product</h2>
    <p>
      There is no chatbot here, no recommendation model, no vector database and
      no runtime language-model call. The "where to go next" suggestions are
      computed from connections that a human wrote down and explained; you can
      read the reason on every card.
    </p>
    <p>
      AI is used privately, as a research assistant — for finding candidate
      paradoxes, tracking down references, and drafting material that is then
      checked and edited by a person before publication. Nothing reaches this
      site without a human deciding it should.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      The whole site is {catalog.paradoxes.length} Markdown files, some YAML,
      and a static site generator. There is no database and no server. The
      published output is plain files on a CDN, which is why it is fast and
      costs essentially nothing to run.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is also a maintenance decision: adding the next hundred entries is the
      same operation as adding the first, and it involves no code.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Something wrong? It probably is, somewhere. The content is a set of text
      files in version control, which makes being wrong in public fixable rather
      than embarrassing. Corrections with a source attached are the most useful
      thing anyone can send.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .ab { max-width: 38rem; padding-block: 2rem 4rem; }
  .ab h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .ab .lede { margin-bottom: 2rem; }

  .abnat { margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .abnat > div {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
    padding: 0.15rem 0 0.15rem 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .abnat dt {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 620;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .abnat dt em {
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.73rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .abnat dd { margin: 0.2rem 0 0; font-size: 0.93rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
